first_claim:
An optical switching network comprising: 
   switching stages including: 
   an input stage (84) of optical switching elements (90-93), each switching element of said input stage comprising one input port and two output ports and 
   an output stage (88) of optical switch elements (94-101) each switch element of said output stage comprising two input ports and one output port; 
   interconnection regions (80-83) between adjacent ones of said switching stages; 
   and a pattern of optical waveguides for connecting the input ports and the output ports of the switch elements of adjacent ones of said stages, said pattern including optical waveguide crossovers CHARACTERISED IN THAT 
   said switching stages are mounted on a substrate and include at least two additional stages (85-87) of switch elements mounted on said substrate between said input stage and said output stage; 
   in that said pattern of optical waveguides is diffused on said substrate in said interconnection regions, and in that said waveguide crossovers include crossovers diffused in said substrate in the regions (80,83) adjacent to said input and output stages.

all_claims:
An optical switching network comprising: 
   switching stages including: 
   an input stage (84) of optical switching elements (90-93), each switching element of said input stage comprising one input port and two output ports and 
   an output stage (88) of optical switch elements (94-101) each switch element of said output stage comprising two input ports and one output port; 
   interconnection regions (80-83) between adjacent ones of said switching stages; 
   and a pattern of optical waveguides for connecting the input ports and the output ports of the switch elements of adjacent ones of said stages, said pattern including optical waveguide crossovers CHARACTERISED IN THAT 
   said switching stages are mounted on a substrate and include at least two additional stages (85-87) of switch elements mounted on said substrate between said input stage and said output stage; 
   in that said pattern of optical waveguides is diffused on said substrate in said interconnection regions, and in that said waveguide crossovers include crossovers diffused in said substrate in the regions (80,83) adjacent to said input and output stages.

An optical switching network having N input ports and M output ports CHARACTERISED IN THAT M and N are non-zero powers of 2, in that said ports are mounted on a substrate and in that the network comprises: 
   a number of switch stages (84-88) equal to log₂ M plus log₂ N where each stage is assigned a number from 1 to log₂ M plus log₂ N in sequence from the input ports to the output ports; 
   where the stage (86) numbered log₂ M comprises   optical 1 by 2 switch elements (112) and each stage between the stage log₂ M and the input ports comprises one-half of the number of 1 by 2 optical switch elements in the next nearer stage to the stage log₂ M and where the stage (87) numbered (log₂ M)+1 comprises   optical 2 by 1 switch elements (110) and each stage between stage (log₂ M)+1 and the output ports comprises one-half of the number of 2 by 1 optical switch elements in the next nearer stage to the stage (log₂ M)+1; 
   interconnection means (82) for interconnecting the M × N outputs of stage log₂ M to the M×N inputs of stage (log₂ M)+1; 
   input interconnection means (80,81) for connecting the outputs and inputs of adjacent stages of the stages numbered 1 through log₂ M; 
   output interconnection means (83) for connecting the outputs and inputs of adjacent stages of the stages numbered (log₂ M)+1 through log₂ M plus log₂ N; 
   wherein the connections between at least one of the stages connected by said input and said output interconnection means (83) comprise optical crossovers of optical waveguides diffused in said substrate.

The method of designing an optical switching network having N input ports and M output ports CHARACTERISED IN THAT M and N are non-zero powers of 2 and N ≦ M, in that said ports are mounted to a substrate and in that the method comprises: 
   defining a number of switch stages (84-88) equal to log₂ M plus log₂ N where each stage is mounted to said substrate and assigned a number from 1 to log₂ M plus log₂ N in sequence from the input ports to the output ports, adjacent ones of said stages being separated by regions, and stage log₂ M and (log₂ M)+1 being separated by a center region; 
   defining a number of optical switch elements for each of the stages, where the stage (86) numbered log₂ M comprises   optical 1 by 2 switch elements (112) and each stage between the stage log₂ M and the input ports comprises one-half of the number of 1 by 2 optical switch elements in the next nearer stage to the stage log₂ M and where the stage (87) numbered (log₂ M)+1 comprises   optical 2 by 1 switch elements (110) and each stage between stage (log₂ M)+1 and the output ports comprises one-half of the number of 2 by 1 optical switch elements in the next nearer stage to the stage (log₂ M)+1; 
   defining a sequence of regions comprising a first region (80) adjacent to the stage (84) having the smallest number of switch elements and continuing toward the center region (82) for (log₂ M) - (log₂ N) regions and then alternating between the farthest output region (83) from the center region not yet in the sequence and the farthest input region from the center region not yet in the sequence until all regions except the center region have been placed in the sequence; 
   defining a pattern of connections for each region in the defined sequence by selecting the regions in the defined sequence, 
   dividing the stages on each side of the selected region into groups of switch elements wherein the number of groups in each stage is given by   times the number of switch elements in the stage, where i is the number of prior regions for which a pattern of connection has been determined, and where each group in a stage corresponds to a group in an adjacent stage and each switch element has a unique position within the group; and 
   determining a pattern of connections between the switch elements of the two stages separated by the selected region such that each switch element having a given position in a given group in the stage farther from the center region is connected to a switch element having the same group position in the corresponding group of the adjacent stage nearer to the center region and to a switch element in the corresponding group having a position equal to the given position plus one-half of the number of switch elements in the corresponding group; and 
   determining a pattern of connections between the switch elements of adjacent stages separated by the center region after all other regions have had a pattern of connections determined.

The method of designing an optical switching network having X input ports and Y output ports where X < Y CHARACTERISED IN THAT said ports are mounted to a substrate and in that the method comprises 
   increasing X to a number N, when X is not a power of 2, N being the next highest power of 2 to X; 
   increasing Y to a number M, when Y is not a power of 2, M being the next highest power of 2 to Y; 
   defining a number of switch stages (84-88) equal to log₂ M plus log₂ N where each stage is mounted to said substrate and assigned a number from 1 to log₂ M plus log₂ N in sequence from the input ports to the output ports, adjacent ones of said stages being separated by regions (81-83), and stage log₂ M and (log₂ M)+1 being separated by a center region (82); 
   defining a number of optical switch elements for each of the stages, where the stage numbered log₂ M comprises   optical 1 by 2 switch elements and each sage between the stage log₂ M and the input ports comprises one-half of the number of 1 by 2 optical switch elements in the next nearer stage to the stage log₂ M and where the stage (log₂ M)+1 comprises   optical 2 by 1 switch elements and each stage between stage (log₂ M)+1 and the output ports comprises one-half of the number of 2 by 1 optical switch elements in the next nearer stage to the stage (log₂ M)+1; 
   defining a sequence of regions comprising a first region adjacent to the stage having the smallest number of switch elements and continuing toward the center region for (log₂ M)-(log₂ N) regions and then alternating between the farthest output region from the center region not yet in the sequence and the farthest input region from the center region not yet in the sequence until all regions except the center region have been placed in the sequence; 
   defining a pattern of connections for each region in the defined sequence by selecting the regions in the defined sequence, 
   dividing the stages on each side of the selected region into groups of switch elements wherein the number of groups in each stage is given by   times the number of switch elements in the stage, where  i  is the number of prior regions for which a pattern of connection has been determined, and where each group in a stage corresponds to a group in a adjacent stage and each switch element has a unique position with the group; and 
   determining a pattern of connections between the switch elements of the two stages separated by the selected region such that each switch element having a given position in a given group in the stage farther from the center region is connected to a switch element having the same group position in the corresponding group of the adjacent stage nearer to the center stage and to a switch element in the corresponding group having a position equal to the given position plus one-half of the number of switch elements in the corresponding group; 
   determining a pattern of connections between the switch elements of the adjacent stages separated by the center region after all other regions have had a pattern of connections determined; 
   deleting N-X switch elements in stage number 1 and deleting each switch element in stages numbered 2 through log₂ M which is connected to receive signals from the deleted switch elements in stage 1; and 
   deleting M-Y switch elements in stage (log₂ M)+(log₂ N) and deleting each switch element in stages (log₂ M)+1 to (log₂ M)+(log₂ N)-1 which is connected to provide signals to the switch elements deleted from stage (log₂ M)+(log₂ N).
